WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.970
Hey, David does a lot of discussion about
taxes, wealth tax, capital gains, tax income

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.810
tax question.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:10.710
Can you just tell us what you think the top
marginal tax rates should be?

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.860
Yes, I can.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.360
I think it should be between 70 and 73%.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:23.330
Um, now I am not suggesting that at a $500,000
a year in income where the question that I

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:30.380
have is at what income level should a 70%
marginal tax rate kick in?

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.900
It shouldn't be after you earn $50,000.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.280
I don't believe it should be after you earn
$150,000 I don't believe it should be.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.429
Once you're earning $500,000, I think that
we should get to 70 or 73%.

00:00:41.429 --> 00:00:48.710
The overwhelming majority of the research
I've seen shows that if scaled correctly,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:56.089
topping out at 70 or 73 generates the most
government revenue before you start losing

00:00:56.089 --> 00:01:02.230
revenue because the tax is so high that people
start getting disincentivized from work.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:07.910
So I've seen proposals that kick in the 70%
at $1 million a year of income.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:13.510
I've seen proposals that do it at $2 million
a year of income or $5 million a year of income

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.180
or $10 million a year of income, maybe 10
millions too high.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.330
That really is a very small percentage of
the population, but there are two things to

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:21.390
keep in mind.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:27.090
One, uh, it's the marginal tax rate below
that level of income, wherever you set it,

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.000
you are paying a lower rate.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.310
And number two, there should be more rates
between our current top rate.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:40.170
I believe the current top rate is 37% there
needs to be more between 37 and 70 if that

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:45.630
were to be kicked in, so it would be graduated,
you might go, okay, I think right now for

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.250
20 or four 80 maybe is where 37% kicks in.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:54.590
Then you might go to 45 between four 80 and
seven 50 or a million.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.770
Then you might go to 52 between 1,000,002
million.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:02.570
It's got a scale and those are the models
that generate the most government revenue

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.870
and they are not so oppressive that they start
to disincentivize work.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.629
In a, that will be problematic for the economy.

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:15.410
A lot of research on this, I'm always up to
having my mind changed on it.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:20.069
Now there's other questions that are ancillary
to this that are important ones.

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:26.189
Like for example, should capital gains be
taxed at that same rate?

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:33.099
Right now there's a lot of very wealthy people
who uh, if they were to pay income tax as

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:40.099
a, as earned income as wages would be paying
37%, but because they are receiving a lot

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:45.129
of this income through capital gains, whether
it's dividends or other sorts of capital gains,

00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:49.720
selling stocks that they've made money on,
um, they are taxed at the lower capital gains

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:50.720
rates.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.810
So one idea that is often mentioned is in
order to eliminate that, you really need to

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:02.499
raise the capital gains rate to simply be
what the, uh, earned income, the, the wage

00:03:02.499 --> 00:03:07.069
rate would be personal income tax rates, so
that there is no advantage to earning the

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:13.599
income via capital gains rather than working
for a living as, as some like to say I'm open,

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:17.999
I'm open to raising, I believe capital gains
should be raised, but I think you also need

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:24.069
more levels previously or right now I believe
that there's just two rates for capital gains.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:25.879
I think it should be progressive.

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:29.359
It needs to have more tiers than two.

00:03:29.359 --> 00:03:32.230
And I'm getting, I'm going from memory here,
I'm, I'm just riffing.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:39.140
Um, but I do think that there are more delicate
considerations when you raise capital gains.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:42.700
I think it should be done, but I think we
need to understand that it is not the same

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.129
as raising taxes on a wages.

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:51.609
But to go back to the beginning, I believe
the top marginal tax rates should be 70%.

00:03:51.609 --> 00:03:55.999
The debate is at what level of income should
that rate kick in.

00:03:55.999 --> 00:03:58.680
And I want to hear from you, uh, on this issue.

